-- RSS guard for the per-ELEMENT float box leak in the compiled
-- NativeArray.fold_float / fold_f32 runtime helpers.
--
-- native_float_arr_fold / native_f32_arr_fold materialise each stored
-- element into a FRESH march_alloc_float box before calling the closure
-- (the wire ABI passes a Float argument as an erased boxed pointer). The
-- closure treats that argument as borrowed and never releases it, so
-- without the explicit `march_decrc(elem)` after the call the box leaks:
-- ~32 B per ELEMENT, unbounded in array length. An output diff cannot see
-- this -- the fold returns the right number either way -- so RSS is the
-- only signal, hence this probe. (native_arr_fold.march remains the
-- correctness/parity witness; this file guards only memory.)
--
-- ACCUMULATOR TYPE IS LOAD-BEARING. There is a SECOND, still-open leak in
-- these same helpers: when the accumulator is itself a Float, each
-- iteration's march_alloc_float result becomes the next accumulator and is
-- never released either (~32 B/element on top of the element box) --
-- inherited from the reference march_typed_array_fold and tracked in
-- specs/todos/2026-08-13-native-array-fold-accumulator-chain-leak.md. An
-- INT accumulator is wire-tagged, never heap-allocated, so it contributes
-- zero bytes and this probe measures the element box in ISOLATION. Do not
-- "simplify" either fold body to `acc +. x` -- that would make the rule
-- fail for the OTHER, known-open leak and destroy its ability to pin this
-- one.
--
-- Both legs are needed: the two decrc(elem) calls are separate lines in
-- separate C functions, and deleting either one alone must go RED. Each leg
-- is its own pfn, but note that freeing an array does NOT hand pages back to
-- the OS, so peak RSS is the SUM of the two arrays (32 MB f64 + 16 MB f32),
-- not the max. That is accounted for in the threshold below.
--
-- Numbers, measured on this fixture at 4,000,000 elements per leg (Darwin
-- arm64, --opt 2). Both sabotage directions were actually run, not reasoned:
--   both decrc present       peak RSS =  50,397,184 B ( 48.1 MB)
--   f64 decrc(elem) deleted  peak RSS = 178,896,896 B (170.6 MB)
--   f32 decrc(elem) deleted  peak RSS = 178,864,128 B (170.6 MB)
-- Each sabotage adds 128.5 MB == 32.1 B x 4,000,000, i.e. exactly one leaked
-- 32-byte march_alloc_float cell per element. The dune rule asserts < 96 MB:
-- 2.0x above the healthy figure (ample allocator/GC noise headroom) and 1.8x
-- below either leaked figure, so it is falsifiable in both directions.
--
-- Init accumulator is 1 (ODD) on purpose, for the same reason as
-- native_arr_fold.march: an even init can hide a lost
-- builtin_boxed_generic_params_tbl entry behind the conditional untag.
--
-- .expected IS interpreter-produced (unlike simd_leak_probe's, which is
-- arithmetic): the tree-walker completes this fixture in ~7 minutes, slow but
-- finite, so compiled/interpreted parity here is a real observation. Do not
-- scale the element count up much further without rechecking that.
--
-- The probe prints its own peak RSS to STDERR via IO.warn so stdout stays
-- diffable while the dune rule reads the RSS figure out of the captured
-- stderr file -- same three-rule structure as simd_leak_probe (run / stdout
-- diff / threshold assertion).
mod NativeArrFoldLeakProbe do
  needs IO.Console

  -- Int accumulator: counts the positive elements. Reads x (so the element
  -- box really is consumed) without ever holding a Float in acc.
  pfn f64_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_float(4000000, 1.0)
    NativeArray.fold_float(arr, 1, fn (acc, x) ->
      if x > 0.0 do acc + 1 else acc end)
  end

  pfn f32_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_f32(4000000, 1.0)
    NativeArray.fold_f32(arr, 1, fn (acc, x) ->
      if x > 0.0 do acc + 1 else acc end)
  end

  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    println(f64_leg())
    println(f32_leg())
    IO.warn(int_to_string(peak_rss_bytes()))
  end
end
